data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration is needed here.**

Your code only uses `Thread.Start()`, `Thread.Join()`, and `Thread.Sleep()` — none of which are affected by the .NET 6+ breaking change. These APIs work identically on .NET 8. The code will compile and run correctly as-is on `net8.0`.

---

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort migration):

If you'd like to modernize the parallelism, you could replace manual threads with `Parallel.ForEach`:

```csharp
public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
{
    Parallel.ForEach(items, item =>
    {
        Thread.Sleep(100);
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    });
}
```

Or with `Task.WhenAll` if you want async support and cancellation readiness:

```csharp
public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken token = default)
{
    var tasks = items.Select(item => Task.Run(() =>
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }, token));

    await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
}
```

These are **quality-of-life improvements** (better thread pool usage, cancellation support) — not required for the migration to succeed.
